WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.372
The next day Charley and the Dodger went out to work as usual.

2
00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:08.864
Fagin took the opportunity to lecture Oliver on the sin of ingratitude.

3
00:00:08.865 --> 00:00:12.936
The old man clearly thought Oliver was guilty of that sin.

4
00:00:12.937 --> 00:00:22.785
"Remember who took you in, my dear," said Fagin. "I cherished you. Without my timely aid, you might have perished from hunger."

5
00:00:22.786 --> 00:00:32.690
Fagin smiled hideously and patted Oliver's head. "But if you behave and apply yourself to business, there's a chance we'll still be very good friends."

6
00:00:32.691 --> 00:00:35.602
Oliver did not want to be friends with Fagin.

7
00:00:35.603 --> 00:00:43.436
Taking his hat and wrapping himself in an old, patched coat, Fagin left the room. Oliver heard him lock the door.

8
00:00:43.437 --> 00:00:48.587
And so Oliver remained all that day and for many more days.

9
00:00:48.588 --> 00:00:52.595
He saw no one between the early morning hours and midnight.

10
00:00:52.596 --> 00:01:02.325
He was alone with his thoughts, and those always turned toward his kind friends—Mr. Brownlow and Mrs. Bedwin. Oliver was miserable indeed.

11
00:01:02.326 --> 00:01:08.904
After about a week, Fagin left the door to the room unlocked, so Oliver was free to wander about the house.

12
00:01:08.905 --> 00:01:16.896
Looking at the ornate fireplaces and intricately carved woodwork, Oliver concluded that the house had once belonged to better people.

13
00:01:16.897 --> 00:01:25.809
But now it was a dismal and dreary place. Spiders had built their webs in the corners, and mice sometimes scampered across the floor.

14
00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:32.150
The shutters were closed tight over all the windows, so only tiny cracks of light entered the rooms.

15
00:01:32.151 --> 00:01:41.801
On the top floor, there was one room with bars over the window instead of shutters. Oliver often gazed out this window for hours at a time.

16
00:01:41.802 --> 00:01:46.872
But all he could see were the roofs of other houses and their blackened chimney tops.

17
00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:50.705
One afternoon Charley and the Dodger were making plans.

18
00:01:50.706 --> 00:01:59.959
"Oh, Oliver," called the Dodger. "My boots need cleaning before I can go out this evening like a proper gent. Come here and clean them."

19
00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:03.872
Oliver was so lonely that he was glad to make himself useful.

20
00:02:03.873 --> 00:02:12.023
The Dodger sat on the kitchen table while Oliver knelt on the floor, holding the Dodger's foot in his lap. Charley stood around and watched.

21
00:02:12.024 --> 00:02:17.856
"Why won't you work for Fagin?" Charley asked as Oliver scraped the mud from the Dodger's left boot.

22
00:02:17.857 --> 00:02:22.015
"You'd make a fortune," said the Dodger, swinging his right leg.

23
00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:30.356
"I don't like it," Oliver said timidly. "I wish he'd let me go. I . . . I'd rather go."

24
00:02:30.357 --> 00:02:34.594
"And Fagin would rather not let you go," said Charley with a laugh.

25
00:02:34.595 --> 00:02:40.340
Oliver knew this was true, but he thought it might be dangerous to express his feelings more openly.

26
00:02:40.341 --> 00:02:43.912
So he only sighed and went on with his boot cleaning.

27
00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:49.420
"Come on," said Charley. "You know we're the only friends you've got now."

28
00:02:49.421 --> 00:02:57.174
"Some friends." Oliver stopped working. "You ran off and let me get punished for something you did."

29
00:02:57.175 --> 00:03:05.309
"Had to," said the Dodger. "The police know we work for Fagin. He might have gotten into trouble if we'd been caught."

30
00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:09.730
Oliver didn't say anything as he finished polishing the Dodger's boots.

31
00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:15.484
"You know what your problem is?" said the Dodger as he admired his shiny black boots.

32
00:03:15.485 --> 00:03:20.476
"You've not been brought up properly. But Fagin will make something of you.

33
00:03:20.477 --> 00:03:27.452
And if you don't steal watches and handkerchiefs, someone else will. So you might as well do it and get the money."

34
00:03:27.453 --> 00:03:35.785
"That's all true," said Fagin, who had entered the room unseen by Oliver. "Now, everyone, come sit by the fire."

35
00:03:35.786 --> 00:03:41.698
The boys pulled some chairs close to the fire, and Fagin insisted that Oliver sit next to him.

36
00:03:41.699 --> 00:03:49.436
Fagin was so charming that night and his stories were so amusing that Oliver found himself laughing heartily despite his better judgment.

37
00:03:49.437 --> 00:03:54.865
From that day Oliver was seldom left alone but spent many hours with the two boys.

38
00:03:54.866 --> 00:04:02.428
They all took turns playing their old game with Fagin, who also entertained them with stories of robberies he'd committed years ago.

39
00:04:02.429 --> 00:04:11.095
For Oliver, anything was better than being left alone with only his own sad thoughts for company. Fagin was slowly winning him over.

40
00:04:11.096 --> 00:04:19.420
One cold, damp night, Fagin left the boys at home while he went out. He had business to discuss with Bill Sikes at his house.

41
00:04:19.421 --> 00:04:24.674
Nancy opened the door. Sikes and his dog were sitting by the fire.

42
00:04:24.675 --> 00:04:32.833
"What's happening with the house in Chertsey? When are you and Toby Crackit going to rob it?" asked Fagin once Nancy had brought him a drink.

43
00:04:32.834 --> 00:04:38.071
Sikes shook his head. "Toby's been hanging about the place for two weeks.

44
00:04:38.072 --> 00:04:44.896
He can't bribe any of the servants to help us. We might still be able to do it, but we'd need a small boy."

45
00:04:44.897 --> 00:04:49.912
"Oh, so you're going to slip him through a window?" asked Fagin.

46
00:04:49.913 --> 00:04:54.643
"Never you mind what we're going to do. Just get me a boy."

47
00:04:54.644 --> 00:04:57.873
"Oliver is a small boy," said Nancy.

48
00:04:57.874 --> 00:05:07.127
"That he is," said Fagin, "and it's time he started working for his bread. He'll do anything you want as long as you frighten him enough."

49
00:05:07.128 --> 00:05:12.889
"Oh, I'll frighten him," said Sikes. "You don't need to worry about that."

50
00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:23.460
"Good!" said Fagin enthusiastically. "Once we fill his mind with the idea that he has been a thief, he's ours. He'll be ours for life!"

51
00:05:23.461 --> 00:05:26.952
Fagin crossed his arms and hugged himself with joy.

52
00:05:26.953 --> 00:05:31.611
"Ours!" said Sikes. "Yours, you mean."

53
00:05:31.612 --> 00:05:33.691
"Perhaps I do."

54
00:05:33.692 --> 00:05:41.016
"And what makes you take such pains with one pale kid when there are fifty boys on the streets of London that you might choose from?"

55
00:05:41.017 --> 00:05:52.058
"Because they're of no use to me. Their looks convict them when they get into trouble. But this boy appears so innocent. If I properly manage him . . ."

56
00:05:52.083 --> 00:05:59.667
Fagin gazed into the fire. "Never mind why I want him. Now when are you going to rob this house in Chertsey?"

57
00:05:59.668 --> 00:06:06.008
After some discussion it was agreed that Nancy would go to Fagin's the next night and bring Oliver back to Sikes.

58
00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:10.596
Oliver would trust her because she had protected him when Fagin wanted to beat him.

59
00:06:10.597 --> 00:06:17.834
The following day they would set out for Chertsey. With their arrangements settled, Fagin went out into the night.

60
00:06:17.835 --> 00:06:21.747
When he reached his gloomy home, the Dodger answered the door.

61
00:06:21.748 --> 00:06:27.993
"Is Oliver in bed? I want to speak to him," said Fagin as they went into the kitchen.

62
00:06:27.994 --> 00:06:31.239
"Here he is," said the Dodger softly.

63
00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:37.888
Oliver was lying fast asleep on the floor. His face was pale with anxiety and sadness.

64
00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:45.595
"Not now, not yet," murmured Fagin. "I'll wait until tomorrow."

